# Tutorial 04 — Images and Backgrounds
# How to declare backgrounds, switch between them, and organize your visual assets.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_04_images
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: Notice the scenery behind me? That's a {b}background{/b} — and you have full control over it.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: This lesson covers how to declare backgrounds, swap them during scenes, and keep your asset folders tidy.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: Pick a topic:
> Declaring backgrounds -> section_declare
> Switching backgrounds with [bg] -> section_switching
> Asset organization tips -> section_organization
> Composing a scene -> section_composing
> Quick reference card -> reference_sheet
> Practice scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Declaring Backgrounds ───────────────────────────────────────

@label section_declare
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Before you can use a background in a scene, you declare it with {b}@background{/b}.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@background field_day assets/backgrounds/field_day.png{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@background classroom assets/backgrounds/school_classroom.png{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The first word is the {b}ID{/b} — a short name you'll use in scripts. The rest is the path to the image file.
Lavender: You can declare backgrounds at the top of any .vns file, or in a shared definitions file.
Lavender: For this project, the demo backgrounds are already declared at the top of each tutorial file via a template variable.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: A good naming convention helps a lot. I like {b}location_timeofday{/b} — "cafe_evening", "park_rain", "bedroom_night".
> Next: Switching backgrounds -> section_switching
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Switching Backgrounds ───────────────────────────────────────

@label section_switching
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Once declared, switching backgrounds mid-scene is one command:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bg field_day]{/color}
Lavender: That instantly replaces whatever's on screen with the "field_day" image.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: For a smoother change, pair it with a {b}[transition]{/b}:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[transition fade 450]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bg classroom]{/color}
Lavender: The transition plays first — a 450ms fade — then the new background appears.
Lavender: We'll cover transitions in depth in Tutorial 05, but the basic combo is worth knowing now.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: You can swap backgrounds as often as you like. Hallway to classroom, day to night, indoors to outdoors — one line each.
> Next: Asset organization -> section_organization
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Asset Organization ──────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_organization
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: A visual novel can accumulate a {i}lot{/i} of images. Getting organized early saves pain later.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Here's a structure that scales well:
narrator: {b}assets/backgrounds/{/b} — all your background images.
narrator: {b}assets/characters/sprites/{/b} — character sprite sheets or individual poses.
narrator: {b}assets/cg/{/b} — full-screen CG illustrations for special moments.
narrator: {b}assets/effects/{/b} — overlays, particles, vignettes.
narrator: {b}assets/ui/{/b} — text box frames, buttons, icons.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Name files consistently. If your background is a cafe at night, call it {b}cafe_night.png{/b}, not {b}IMG_3847.png{/b}.
Lavender: Your future self will thank you when you have fifty backgrounds and need to find one fast.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: The engine doesn't care about folder depth — only the path in your @background declaration matters.
> Next: Composing a scene -> section_composing
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Scene Composition ───────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_composing
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: A background is your canvas. Characters are painted on top of it. Let me show you what that looks like in script.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bg field_day]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender center happy]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: What a beautiful morning!{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Three lines — set the stage, place the character, deliver the dialogue. That's a complete scene beat.
Lavender: You can layer multiple characters on different positions:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender left talking]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show friend right happy]{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Think of it like blocking in theater — who's where, what they look like, and what backdrop they're standing in front of.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Change any of those three elements and the scene feels completely different.
> Reference card -> reference_sheet
> Try it yourself -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Reference Card ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label reference_sheet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Background and image cheat sheet:
narrator: {b}@background id path{/b} — declare a background with an ID and file path.
narrator: {b}[bg id]{/b} — switch to that background immediately.
narrator: {b}[transition fade N]{/b} — fade transition over N milliseconds (use before [bg]).
narrator: {b}[transition crossfade N id]{/b} — crossfade directly to a new background.
narrator: {b}Folder convention:{/b} assets/backgrounds/, assets/characters/, assets/cg/.
narrator: {b}Naming:{/b} location_timeofday — cafe_night, park_rain, school_morning.
narrator: {b}Composition:{/b} [bg] sets the canvas, [show] places characters on it.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Keep declarations close to where they're used, or centralize them in a definitions file — either works.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice Scene ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Let's build a quick scene together. Pick the setting:
> Morning in a field -> practice_field
> Inside a cozy room -> practice_room
> Somewhere mysterious -> practice_mystery

@label practice_field
[show lavender center happy]
narrator: Golden light stretched across the meadow. The grass swayed gently in the breeze.
Lavender: Ah, this is the best time of day. Everything's so warm.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Open this file and look at how the background was set at the top — that's all it takes.
Lavender: Try changing the background declaration to point to a different image and see what happens.
[set practice_result 1]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_room
[show lavender center happy]
narrator: Bookshelves lined every wall. A reading lamp cast a warm circle of amber light on the desk.
Lavender: I could spend all day in a place like this.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Indoor scenes work great with characters placed slightly off-center — it feels more natural.
Lavender: Try adding a [show] command with "left" or "right" instead of "center" and see how it changes the feel.
[set practice_result 2]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_mystery
[hide lavender]
[wait 500]
narrator: The corridor was longer than it should have been. The overhead lights buzzed and flickered.
[wait 400]
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: ...I don't remember this hallway being here before.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Mysterious scenes benefit from hiding the character first, letting the background speak for itself, then revealing them.
[set practice_result 3]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_wrap
[show lavender center happy]
[if practice_result == 1]
Lavender: Outdoor scenes feel open and inviting. Let the narrator paint what the background image shows.
[elif practice_result == 2]
Lavender: Indoor scenes are intimate. Character placement and lighting descriptions do the heavy lifting.
[else]
Lavender: Mystery scenes thrive on negative space. An empty stage with just a background is powerful.
[endif]
Lavender: Swap in your own background art and these same scripts will feel totally different. That's the magic of it.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_1]
